WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.060
Donald Trump’s pitch to Make America Great Again

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:11.680
included a commitment to rethink our interventionist
foreign policy.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:15.900
We went from mistakes in Iraq, to Egypt, to
Libya

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.580
to President Obama's line in the sand in Syria.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:24.140
Each of these actions have helped to throw
the region into chaos.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:27.360
Trump’s promise to unwind America’s foreign
commitments

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.180
won the vote of some anti-war libertarians

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:34.380
who argued that, while many of his policy
views were odious,

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:37.120
foreign policy mattered most.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:43.200
Donald is a peacenik practically, certainly
compared to the warmongering Hillary.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:48.110
On the campaign trail, Trump attacked Clinton
for pushing for U.S. intervention in Libya

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:49.720
as secretary of state,

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:53.240
and her 2002 vote in the Senate for the Iraq
war.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:59.400
Any vote that might lead to war should be
hard but I cast it with conviction.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:04.540
Almost everything she's done in foreign policy
has been a mistake and it's been a disaster.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.560
Trump would prove to be the less interventionist
alternative to Clinton

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:13.700
argued historian Thaddeus Russel in a 2016
interview with Reason.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.140
Whenever there's a dictator or tyrant we don't
like in any part of the world

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.660
we are obligated to remove them.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:23.500
Trump is the first President to call bullshit
on that very claim.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:28.420
It all began with the dangerous idea that
we could make western democracies

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:35.040
out of countries that had no experience or
interest in becoming a western democracy.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.860
And in doing so I think he may do great service for actual peace believe it or not.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:43.120
But has Trump lived up to the non-interventionist
hype?

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:47.680
I think Trump has moved America considerably
closer to peace

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:54.940
at the very same time he's moved us into more
wars, so it's a terribly mixed bag.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:02:00.020
Russel says that Trump's rhetoric alone advanced
the non-interventionist cause.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:04.460
He called into question the need for America
to invade countries,

00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:08.560
to change their regimes and to stay there
as occupation forces.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.190
Specifically he called into question the Iraq
war.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:15.840
Obviously the war in Iraq was a big fat mistake.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.610
We spent 2 trillion dollars, thousands of
lives, we don't even have it,

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:23.560
we should have never been in Iraq, we have
destabilized the middle east.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:28.690
And that was really important and historic
and I'm really grateful for that.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.200
Unfortunately of course no one took him seriously,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.040
or they took him extremely seriously and tried
to bring him down.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.900
Trump isn’t the first modern president

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.440
to promise an end to foreign entanglements on the campaign trail

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.410
only to double down on those commitments once
in office.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:49.870
Candidate Barack Obama called Iraq a stupid
war that he would end and never replicate.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.540
We have to use our military wisely

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.980
and we did not use our military wisely in
Iraq.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:02.040
Obama did temporarily withdraw troops from
Iraq on Bush’s pre-negotiated timetable,

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:06.900
but intervened again a few years later after
ISIS filled the power vacuum.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:10.630
He also expanded the war on terror into several
new countries

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:14.210
and began personally ordering covert drone
strikes,

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:16.930
one of which killed a 16-year-old American

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:20.760
and another that killed at least 13 people
headed to a wedding.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:26.340
Even George W. Bush, who started the disastrous
wars in Iraq and Afghanistan,

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.360
ran against nation building on the campaign
trail.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.360
I just don't think it's the role of the United
States to roll into a country say

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.100
'we do it this way, so should you.'

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:38.530
We know the American people want peace.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:42.060
Scott Horton, a popular antiwar podcast host

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:45.920
and author of a book on the history of the
War in Afghanistan,

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:51.070
agrees with Russell that Trump’s rhetorical
attack on the foreign policy establishment

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:53.080
helped the anti-war cause.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:57.450
It was necessary for him to destroy the legacy
of George W. Bush

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:00.320
in order to help to defeat Jeb Bush.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:05.470
he really got the right, the, the vast American
sort of tea party, Republican voters of America

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:09.640
to finally admit that they were wrong to have
supported George Bush.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.220
But he says Trump is too impulsive to be reliably
antiwar.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.680
The problem with Donald Trump of course, is
that he's a millimeter deep,

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.980
the guy, you know, he has some instincts,
but he doesn't have thinking really on these things.

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:24.260
He doesn't actually really care.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:27.789
It's just a matter of sort of what feels stupid
to him or not.

00:04:27.789 --> 00:04:31.659
It seems stupid to pour a bunch of money into
the Afghan pit.

00:04:31.659 --> 00:04:37.430
But is it something for him to have real conviction
about and prevent from happening? No.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:43.310
Trump’s wars with the media, Democrats,
and protesters draw daily press coverage.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:46.980
But just because America's actual wars aren't
on the news every night

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.840
doesn't mean they are any closer to ending.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:52.419
Trump hasn't invaded any new countries.

00:04:52.419 --> 00:04:57.880
But he has ramped up the nearly 19 year long,
2 trillion dollar Afghanistan war

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:05.590
that's cost the lives of tens of thousands
of Afghanis and more than 3500 US and NATO troops.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:14.780
My original instinct was to pull out and historically
I like following my instincts.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:18.820
Once they get in there, all the incentives
are to keep the thing going.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:22.340
Trump added thousands more troops in 2017

00:05:22.349 --> 00:05:28.080
and dropped more bombs and missiles in 2019
than in any previous recorded year.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:35.800
The consequences of a rapid exit are both
predictable and unacceptable.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:41.180
If you look at Barack Obama and Donald Trump,
both, they come in their first year in power

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:46.900
and immediately they're met with this full
court press by the military for an escalation.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:51.380
Instead of withdrawing troops from Afghanistan,
Iraq, and Syria,

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:54.759
he vowed to stop publicly reporting their numbers.

00:05:54.759 --> 00:06:02.490
We will not talk about numbers of troops or
our plans for further military activities.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:07.090
The Trump administration did strike a deal
with Taliban leaders in late February

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:09.810
to wind down the war within 14 months

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:14.280
if they pledged to prevent terrorist groups
like al qaeda from operating in the country.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:17.540
And the Pentagon announced a reduction in
forces

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:22.400
and withdrawal from five Afghanistan  bases on July 14th.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:26.639
The fact that Trump was willing to break with
Bush and Obama's policy

00:06:26.639 --> 00:06:31.010
go ahead and negotiate directly with the Taliban
was a clue that he was really serious.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:33.560
But Horton says that deal could be scuttled

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:38.849
by allegations that Russia paid bounties to
Afghanis who killed US troops,

00:06:38.849 --> 00:06:43.190
which prompted Republican Liz Cheney to partner
with several House Democrats

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:46.260
to place conditions on the Afghanistan exit.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:51.319
The way it's negotiated is okay, we'll withdraw
over the next year.

00:06:51.319 --> 00:06:53.680
If Trump is unelected in the fall,

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:57.960
then it would be the new Biden government,
presumably, uh, to come in

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:01.760
and be in charge of implementing that withdrawal
through next may

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:06.669
And so that's plenty of time, really, not
just for Biden, but even for Trump, if he's reelected

00:07:06.669 --> 00:07:08.379
to go ahead and back down.

00:07:08.379 --> 00:07:12.130
Once he safely reelected, assume one thing
blows up over there.

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:16.120
And then all they have to say is, you know
what circumstances have changed,

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:18.340
whatever it is, and stay.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:23.240
Horton points out that Trump has increased
U.S. involvement in Yemen and Somalia.

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:28.200
These are two of America's most horrible Wars
and no one pays any attention to them whatsoever.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:32.139
Hundreds of thousands of people have died
mostly because of the famine and the difficulties.

00:07:32.139 --> 00:07:37.509
and it is a war designed deliberately to inflict
as much punishment as possible

00:07:37.509 --> 00:07:39.920
on the civilian population of the country,

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:45.280
deliberately targeting water, electricity,
sewage, hospitals, and farms.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:50.960
Anything that is to support the infrastructure
of civilian life in that country.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.460
It's a deliberate medieval siege campaign.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:57.319
And Trump has continued and escalated the
war on terror,

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:02.380
which could make the U.S. susceptible to getting
involved in even more conflicts around the globe.

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:06.000
Any regime in the world that has a problem
with any of their subjects,

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.620
all they have to do is call them terrorists
and ask the Americans for help.

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:12.020
And the Americans will come running.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:15.740
Iran can not be allowed to have a nuclear
weapon.

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:18.800
Trump also undid Obama’s nuclear deal with
Iran

00:08:18.809 --> 00:08:23.669
and ordered a targeted assassination of Iranian
general Qasem Soleimani,

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:27.250
whom he accused of plotting an attack on an
American base.

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:31.210
We took action last night to stop a war.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:34.610
But after Iran fired back, Trump deescalated.

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:38.380
We did not take action to start a war.

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:43.760
The previous year, he’d called off an airstrike
planned as retaliation for a downed drone.

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:45.940
Administration is going to have to respond
to this

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:50.640
or lose a lot of credibility and standing
in the region and around the rest of the world.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:55.540
Look at the narrative and the agenda in the
media.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:58.140
Horton says media figures often criticize
Trump

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:03.020
when he avoids military action or pulls out
troops as he did in northern Syria.

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:07.480
The announcement is seen as another blow to
US allied Kurdish fighters.

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:10.400
Well this is exactly what everyone feared,
a free-for-all

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:13.580
in what was one of the most stable parts in Syria.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:17.250
What he's done is greenlighted Turkey, greenlighted
Russia.

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:23.280
And praise him when he takes military action
such as firing missiles into a Syrian airfield

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:25.550
following reports of a chemical attack.

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:31.490
I am tempted to quote the great Leonard Cohen,
I am guided by the beauty of our weapons.

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:35.800
Donald Trump became the President of the United
States, I think this was actually a big moment.

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:42.700
How dare Donald Trump try to end any of America's
Wars, try to get out of Syria twice.

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:46.190
And they went out full jihad against him to
force him to stay.

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:49.300
And he backed down both times.

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:54.300
One group of scientists believe Trump has
pushed the world closer to nuclear war

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:59.840
citing his withdrawal from the Iranian nuclear
deal and two Russian nuclear treaties

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:04.230
as reason to move the atomic doomsday clock
closer to midnight.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:08.080
And Russell worries about the increasingly
belligerent rhetoric

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:11.280
on both sides of the aisle towards China,

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:14.930
which Trump has escalated tensions with through
his trade war

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:19.440
and reported placement of low-yield nuclear
weapons in the region.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:21.490
Going to war with China seems like an extremely
bad idea.

00:10:21.490 --> 00:10:23.780
I'm actually more worried about the Democrats
longterm.

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:25.240
Hmm, why is that?

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:28.860
They want to surround Russia militarily and
enforce Putin to resign,

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:31.180
back down, offer major concessions

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:34.430
basically do business with the United States
as we see fit.

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:37.030
That's the long term danger but that's not
so much from Trump.

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:41.220
For me the best aspect of the foreign policy
of Trump

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:46.190
is he has revealed the mind of the foreign
policy establishment.

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:51.040
The way that the establishment thinks about
foreign policy and really government workings

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:52.960
and the workings of the state generally.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:56.360
And the worst aspect of the Trump foreign policy?

00:10:56.360 --> 00:11:01.000
Worst aspect of the Trump foreign policy is
that he's a mass murderer, just like the rest of them.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:06.360
In the end Trump had the power as commander
in chief to start bringing American troops home.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:10.500
But he failed to formulate a coherent strategy
for doing so.

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:15.130
We spent $8 trillion over 20 years fighting
all these Wars

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:20.120
and doing nothing but empower our governments,
adversaries the Iranians and their friends

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:24.500
and our real enemies, Al Qaeda and their friends
all around the region.

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:27.820
And meanwhile, you're telling me, we're short
on hospital beds

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:30.520
we're short on masks and gowns for nurses

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:35.890
while you guys are still bombing Iraq for
now, the 30th year straight

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:37.170
it doesn't have to be this way.

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:38.860
It never should have been this way.

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:41.980
We screwed up, got the whole 21st century
off on the wrong foot,

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:44.900
but we didn't need to, we could call the whole
damn thing off now.

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:46.820
Everybody knows that.

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:49.950
And just forge that new consensus and just
stick with it.

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:52.500
It should be easy because we're right.

